{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'Runs that leave work behind|Runs that leave|processes of the run|Every run process|Review runs that leave' POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\"","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
