integration go test ./internal/pose -run TestOnlyReadsThatCannotDependOnMutableStateAreClassifiedPure|TestTheFinalAuditSkipsPureReads|GovernanceReadViewGit -count=1\n  <- audit-pure-git-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build3773734870: read-only file system\n  -> index-input-binding-integration go test ./internal/pose -run TestAnOperationReadsTheTransferPlans -count=1\n  <- index-input-binding-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build274836909: read-only file system\n  -> optional-worktree-runtime-integration go test -race ./internal/worktreeruntime ./internal/cli -run TheIdentityNamesTheWorktree|TheEndpointIsProtected|TheLifecycleDrains|WithNoRuntimeTheClient|TestRuntimeCommands|ConcurrentStartsLeaveOne|AStopAcceptsNothing|RuntimeThatOutlives|AStopReports -count=1\n  <- optional-worktree-runtime-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build2006737149: read-only file system\n  -> claude-sandboxed-profile-integration go test -race ./internal/pose ./internal/cli -run TestTheClaudeAgentDeclaresASandboxedProfile|TestTheSandboxedProfile|TestTheDelegationPolicyReports|TestDelegateAgentsAndDoctorSay -count=1\n  <- claude-sandboxed-profile-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build343267030: read-only file system\n  -> delegate-picker-integration go test -race ./internal/cli -run TestThePickerPreview|TestTheStateGlyph|TestDelegateWatchPicksARun -count=1\n  <- delegate-picker-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build1003538723: read-only file system\n  -> review-brief-templates-are-immutable-integration go test -race ./internal/pose -run TestEveryReviewBriefTemplateVersion|TestTheDigestSeesEverySectionOfEveryVariant -count=1\n  <- review-brief-templates-are-immutable-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build4172258305: read-only file system\n  -> delegated-approval-survives-routine-drift-integration go test -race ./internal/pose -run ADelegatedApproval|ARetainedDelegatedApproval|TheAssuranceOfARetained|ConcludeReviewRunRecordsTheAttestation -count=1\n  <- delegated-approval-survives-routine-drift-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build157382268: read-only file system\n  -> help-lists-every-command-integration go test -race ./internal/cli -run TestPoseHelp|TestTheManualsDocumentEveryCommand|TestTheHelpReaderIgnores -count=1\n  <- help-lists-every-command-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build2494166345: read-only file system\n  -> review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration go test -race ./internal/pose -run TheBriefStatesEveryRule|EveryMessageThePreflight|TheBriefOfALaterRound|AnEarlierConclusionBrief|ReviewBriefIsRenderedFromTheBundle|TheClaudeReviewerProfile -count=1\n  <- review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build4094053175: read-only file system\n  -> done-spec-keeps-its-sealed-plan-integration go test -race ./internal/pose ./internal/cli -run ADoneSpecSealed|AnOpenSpecStillOwes|TheSpecAnswersForTheExitGate|EachExitGateHasItsOwnCriterion|AStopOnAnUnknownView|EveryOtherStopStillRecords|TheStopOnUnstableInputs|AStopWhoseCheckpoint|UnreadableSealedHistory -count=1\n  <- done-spec-keeps-its-sealed-plan-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build1681534048: read-only file system\n  -> common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff-integration go test -race ./internal/pose ./internal/cli ./internal/mcpserver -run InProcessRun|CloseoutHandoff|AHandoff|NoHandoff|PendingJudgment|InProcessWrapper|EveryMCPRoute|OnlyAdmitted|ReadOnlyWrappers|KeepTheProcessBoundary|TypedHandoff|StableAndADone|TheMCPPlanIsTheDomainPlan|AdmittedWritesAreExactly|Golden|BlockedOrUnstable|TheDigestOfAHandoff|ACancelledCaller|ACompletionThat|AbandonedRuns|NotTakenWhenTheSpawn|APanicInAnInProcess|APendingMechanicalStep|TheHandoffKeepsTheSelectedProject|TheHandoffOverMCP -count=1\n  <- common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build3352150544: read-only file system\n  -> generation-bound-query-cursors-integration go test ./internal/pose ./internal/mcpserver -run PageByGeneration|ACursor|HostileCursor|ExpiredCursor|APositionOutside|AContinuation|AnIssuedCursor|EveryPage|PageLimitIsBounded|EquivalentFilters|APageStates|APageCarries|PageCost|DoesNotReverify|SymlinkTarget|PreservesSizeAndModTime|NullCursor|FiltersThatSerialize|ACursorAlias|OmittedSort|AnAlias|AnExplicit|Pagination|ListToolsShareThePaginationSchema -count=1\n  <- generation-bound-query-cursors-integration fail 0.0s exit=1\n      go: creating work dir: mkdir /var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-3562965836/go-build2948517105: read-only file system\n[time] wall 2.7s, checks 20.9s, jobs 8\n[time] 0.2s pose-mcp/release-test-compile-darwin\n[time] 0.1s pose-mcp/vet\n[time] 0.1s pose-mcp/closeout-review-policy-integration\n[time] 0.1s pose-mcp/policy-scope-validation-integration\n[time] 0.1s pose-mcp/fresh-install-health-integration\n233 step(s) · 233 fail · 2.6s\nResult: FAILURE — required check failed\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba POSE.md | sed -n '990,1010p'; nl -ba POSE.md | sed -n '1070,1084p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md | sed -n '990,1008p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md | sed -n '1059,1072p'; nl -ba locales/pt-BR/POSE.md | sed -n '990,1004p'; nl -ba locales/pt-BR/POSE.md | sed -n '1070,1078p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba POSE.md | sed -n '990,1010p'; nl -ba POSE.md | sed -n '1070,1084p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md | sed -n '990,1008p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md | sed -n '1059,1072p'; nl -ba locales/pt-BR/POSE.md | sed -n '990,1004p'; nl -ba locales/pt-BR/POSE.md | sed -n '1070,1078p'\"","aggregated_output":"   990\t  identified risk, contract or finding, with a bounded scope. Qualification of a\n   991\t  separate engine-wide pilot is not an automatic checklist for every spec.\n   992\t- Track machine time and CPU/RAM separately from model input/output and cached\n   993\t  tokens. Do not infer billed cost or unique context from cumulative token counts.\n   994\t\n   995\tUse these practices within the existing review plan and task workflow. They do\n   996\tnot create another mandatory rehearsal, report or approval layer. Keep the\n   997\ttechnical details of execution routing in the sections below.\n   998\t\n   999\t### Bound delegated adapter execution\n  1000\t\n  1001\tDeclare an optional `containment` object on each agent in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`. Use containment schema 1 and `trusted-process`. This backend runs a trusted local executable; a disposable worktree shares Git metadata and does not isolate filesystem, original repository, network, credential files, CPU or memory. Required guarantees that the host cannot enforce are refused before reserving an attempt or spawning a process. Linux process-group termination requires an observable process inventory and is checked after kill; descendants that escape the group remain outside its coverage. Windows/other ports do not qualify group containment from a cross-build.\n  1002\t\n  1003\t```json\n  1004\t{\n  1005\t  \"containment\": {\n  1006\t    \"schema_version\": 1,\n  1007\t    \"profile\": \"trusted-process\",\n  1008\t    \"required\": [\"bounded-capture\", \"environment-allowlist\"],\n  1009\t    \"environment\": [\"LANG\"],\n  1010\t    \"credentials\": [\"REVIEWER_API_KEY\"],\n  1070\tPreview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then add `--apply` to publish an immutable proof under `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL is optional (no expiry when absent), positive when supplied and at most 365 days. Verify it with `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. The proof signs the captured executor declaration; it is not an independent vendor receipt or human confirmation. Failed or interrupted audit records disclose their state and `complete_review=false`. Unknown or ambiguous draft decisions also keep that flag false. A true flag describes completed delivery with one draft decision, not approval or complete typed-conclusion validation. A completed run with incomplete stdin or truncated output cannot establish a completed review. Legacy runs have no captured provenance and cannot be upgraded by signing today's configuration. `pose review run-proof` and the issuer lookups of the verdict readers ask the question the closeout asks: for a run whose scope is done, the proof as it was given (the record stands, the issuer still holds its pin; routine drift of the repository and the proof's expiry are not asked, because the expiry bounds when a proof may back a new attestation, not whether an attestation already given to a done scope stands); for an open scope, as the repository is now.\n  1071\t\n  1072\tAttach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n  1073\t\n  1074\t**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n  1075\t\n  1076\t**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n  1077\t\n  1078\t**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n  1079\t\n  1080\t**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n  1081\t\n  1082\tOpt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\n  1083\t\n  1084\tRead the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\n   990\t```json\n   991\t{\n   992\t  \"containment\": {\n   993\t    \"schema_version\": 1,\n   994\t    \"profile\": \"trusted-process\",\n   995\t    \"required\": [\"bounded-capture\", \"environment-allowlist\"],\n   996\t    \"environment\": [\"LANG\"],\n   997\t    \"credentials\": [\"REVIEWER_API_KEY\"],\n   998\t    \"stream_bytes\": 8388608,\n   999\t    \"output_bytes\": 65536,\n  1000\t    \"retained_bytes\": 16777216,\n  1001\t    \"retention_ttl_seconds\": 0\n  1002\t  }\n  1003\t}\n  1004\t```\n  1005\t\n  1006\tKeep environment/credentials lists unique ignoring case (at most 128/32 names). Ambient token variables are excluded. Explicit profiles use a private disposable HOME; an omitted profile is disclosed as `legacy-trusted` and retains HOME compatibility. This is environment filtering, not protection against an executable reading same-user files. Authorize credential variable names explicitly; never put their values in policy, argv, brief or notes. Loader hooks and Git redirection variables cannot be allowlisted. Credential values and captured session references are redacted together in one bounded pass, longest match first; this is exact-value redaction, not general data-loss prevention or a guarantee against encoded secrets emitted by an untrusted adapter.\n  1007\t\n  1008\tUse a positive timeout from 1ms to 24h (omitted: 60m). Omit `max_runs_per_bundle` for a finite default of three; explicit values must be 1..100, including on legacy adapters. Explicit zero/null is invalid. Omitted stream/output/retained limits are 8MiB/64KiB/16MiB; explicit ranges are 1..64MiB, 1..128KiB and 1..128MiB. Require retained bytes to cover `stream_bytes + 6*output_bytes + 1048576` for JSON escaping and metadata. The retained budget covers the run record plus transcript per execution; independent bounded ledger/proof inventories still apply. It is not an aggregate storage quota, an adapter memory quota or an OS disk limit. Memory/CPU settings, positive TTL and unknown enforcement fields are refused by this backend.\n  1059\tAttach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n  1060\t\n  1061\t**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n  1062\t\n  1063\t**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n  1064\t\n  1065\t**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n  1066\t\n  1067\t**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n  1068\t\n  1069\tOpt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\n  1070\t\n  1071\tRead the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\n   990\t  deduza custo faturado ou contexto único de contagens cumulativas de tokens.\n   991\t\n   992\tAplique estas práticas no review-plan e no workflow existentes. Elas não criam\n   993\toutra camada obrigatória de ensaio, relatório ou aprovação. Consulte nas seções\n   994\tseguintes os detalhes técnicos de roteamento e execução.\n   995\t\n   996\t### Limitar a execução do adapter delegado\n   997\t\n   998\tDeclare `containment` opcional em cada agente de `.pose/policy/delegation.json`, contenção schema 1 e `trusted-process`. O backend exige executável local confiável. Worktree compartilha Git; não isola filesystem, raiz original, rede, credenciais em disco, CPU ou memória. Exigência indisponível é recusada antes da reserva/spawn. Linux qualifica término do grupo somente com inventário observável e verificação após kill. Filho que escapa do grupo fica fora dessa cobertura; cross-build não qualifica runtime Windows/outras plataformas.\n   999\t\n  1000\t```json\n  1001\t{\n  1002\t  \"containment\": {\n  1003\t    \"schema_version\": 1,\n  1004\t    \"profile\": \"trusted-process\",\n  1070\t\n  1071\t**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n  1072\t\n  1073\t**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n  1074\t\n  1075\t**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali os processos do mesmo usuário anteriores ao run cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido (um iniciado durante o run que não pode ser lido derruba a limpeza); nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n  1076\t\n  1077\t**Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\n  1078\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
